// A program that declares no specification of its own and links a library that
// does. Everything in its proof artifact's verification sections therefore came
// from adoption, which is what makes it the fixture that shows a merge
// synthesizing both sections rather than extending ones the program already
// carried.
//
// `scale` has no body here — it arrives from
// `spec_adopted_extern_mathlib.inf` (or, for the tests about obligations a link
// cannot carry, from `spec_adopted_reach_mathlib.inf`), compiled separately in
// proof mode and spliced in by the static-merge linker under the name section
// symbol `mathlib::scale`. The library's own obligation about `scale` is
// rewritten onto that symbol when the link adopts it.
//
// The weight on `b` is a second fingerprint constant, distinct from the one the
// library carries. It is what makes an assertion that reads a body out of the
// module record by ordinal discriminating: a merged artifact with one
// constant-bearing body would satisfy such an assertion no matter which ordinal
// the obligation named.

external fn scale(x: i32) -> i32;
use { scale } from mathlib;

pub fn scaled_sum(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
  return scale(a) + b * 31;
}
